    "thm-associative-symmetrization-satisfies-jordan-identity": {
      "risk": "medium",
      "kind": "theorem",
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-associative-jordan-product",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $k$ be a field with $\\operatorname{char}(k)\\ne2$ and let $A$ be a unital\nassociative $k$-algebra as in [[def-algebra-over-a-commutative-ring]]. On the\nunderlying $k$-vector space define\n\n$$ a\\circ b:=\\frac12(ab+ba). $$\n\nDenote this algebra with product by $A^+$. The product is symmetric and\nbilinear. This definition alone does not assert the Jordan identity; the\nfollowing theorem proves it. It does not assert that a Jordan product is\nassociative.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-algebra-over-a-commutative-ring",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $R$ be a commutative ring ([[def-commutative-ring]]). An **$R$-algebra** is a unital ring $A$ together with a unital ring homomorphism\n\n$$\\eta_A:R\\longrightarrow A$$\n\n([[def-ring-homomorphism]]) whose image is central: $\\eta_A(r)a=a\\eta_A(r)$ for every $r\\in R$ and $a\\in A$. The induced scalar action is $ra:=\\eta_A(r)a$, making $A$ an $R$-module and multiplication $A\\times A\\to A$ bilinear.\n\nAn **$R$-algebra homomorphism** $f:A\\to B$ is a unital ring homomorphism satisfying $f\\circ\\eta_A=\\eta_B$. Such a map is automatically $R$-linear. An $R$-algebra is **commutative** when its underlying ring is commutative.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-jordan-algebra",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $k$ be a field with $\\operatorname{char}(k)\\ne2$ ([[def-field]]) and let\n$J$ be a $k$-vector space ([[def-vector-space]]). A **Jordan $k$-algebra** is a\nbilinear product $\\circ:J\\times J\\to J$ (linear in each argument) such that\n$x\\circ y=y\\circ x$ and\n\n$$ x\\circ(y\\circ x^2)=(x\\circ y)\\circ x^2 $$\n\nfor all $x,y\\in J$, where $x^2:=x\\circ x$. Associativity is not required. The\nalgebra is **unital** if there is an element $1_J$ such that\n$1_J\\circ x=x$ for every $x\\in J$.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-involutive-complex-associative-algebra",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A **complex associative algebra with involution** is a unital associative\nalgebra over $\\mathbb C$ as in [[def-algebra-over-a-commutative-ring]], equipped\nwith a conjugate-linear map $a\\mapsto a^*$ using complex conjugation from\n[[def-complex-conjugate-real-imaginary-part-and-modulus]] such that\n$(a^*)^*=a$, $(ab)^*=b^*a^*$, and $1^*=1$. Its **self-adjoint part** is\n\n$$ A_{\\rm sa}:=\\{a\\in A:a^*=a\\}. $$\n\nThe theorem [[thm-associative-symmetrization-satisfies-jordan-identity]] proves\nthat this subset is a real vector subspace closed under the symmetrized\nproduct.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "By [F1] and the bilinearity of multiplication in [F2], $\\circ$ is bilinear; swapping $a,b$ leaves $ab+ba$ unchanged, so it is commutative. Also $a^2_{\\circ}:=a\\circ a=aa$ because $2$ is invertible in $k$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
